{"answer":"For x≠2, x²−4=(x−2)(x+2), so (x²−4)/(x−2)=x+2. The original expression has domain ℝ\\{2}. The simplified expression x+2 is defined for every real x, so it is not equivalent to the original at every real number: at x=2 it has value 4 while the original expression is undefined. They agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator as a difference of squares, then cancel the common factor only where the denominator is nonzero.","id":"factor-cancel","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This gives a direct self-contained simplification and preserves the excluded input."},{"description":"Treat x+2 as globally equivalent after cancellation.","id":"global-cancel","outcome":"rejected","reason":"Cancellation is valid only for x−2≠0; moreover x+2 is defined at 2 whereas the original quotient is not."}],"change_log":["Used an independent symbolic proof; the supplied computation requests failed and are not used as evidence."],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the difference-of-squares expansion in factor-identity.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"factorization","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x, x²−4=(x−2)(x+2).","step_ids":["factor-identity"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the denominator of the original quotient in original-domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of (x²−4)/(x−2) is ℝ\\{2}.","step_ids":["denominator-condition","original-domain"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by conditional cancellation using factorization and x≠2.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["factorization","original-domain"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"restricted-simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x in the original domain, (x²−4)/(x−2)=x+2.","step_ids":["factor-identity","conditional-cancellation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by evaluating the simplified expression and comparing its domain with the original domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain","restricted-simplification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"not-global-equivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The simplified expression x+2 is not equivalent to the original quotient at every real number; they agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}.","step_ids":["simplified-domain","counterexample-two","agreement-domain"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"factor-identity","justification":"Expanding (x−2)(x+2) gives x²+2x−2x−4=x²−4.","statement":"Establish x²−4=(x−2)(x+2) for every real x."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"denominator-condition","justification":"A real quotient a/b is defined only when b≠0. Here b=x−2, which is zero exactly when x=2.","statement":"The original quotient is defined only if x≠2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["denominator-condition"],"id":"original-domain","justification":"When x≠2, the denominator is nonzero and the polynomial numerator is defined; when x=2, the denominator is zero.","statement":"Conclude that the original domain is ℝ\\{2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-identity","denominator-condition"],"id":"conditional-cancellation","justification":"For x≠2, substitute (x−2)(x+2) for the numerator and cancel the nonzero factor x−2, leaving x+2.","statement":"On the original domain, simplify (x²−4)/(x−2) to x+2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"simplified-domain","justification":"x+2 is a polynomial, and polynomials are defined for all real inputs.","statement":"The simplified expression x+2 is defined at every real number, including 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["simplified-domain","original-domain"],"id":"counterexample-two","justification":"At x=2, x+2=4, while the original quotient is undefined because its denominator is zero.","statement":"Exhibit x=2 as an input at which the two expressions do not have the same defined value."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["conditional-cancellation","counterexample-two"],"id":"agreement-domain","justification":"Conditional-cancellation proves agreement for every x≠2, and counterexample-two shows failure of global equivalence at the only excluded real input.","statement":"The expressions agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}, not at every real number."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
